  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nAn upper-level trough over the western CONUS tracks a low pressure \ncenter through the Northern Plains today. Out ahead of the low \npressure center, a southerly low-level jet will move over part of \nnortheastern South Dakota and western Minnesota this afternoon. The \nstrongest winds are expected to be off to the east of the forecast \narea, but 30-40 knot winds at 850mb will still clip the area. There \nmay be some difficulty in these strongest winds being able to mix to \nthe surface given cloud cover limiting daytime heating, therefore \nalso limiting the ability for a well-mixed boundary layer to \ndevelop. Still, gusts up to 30-40 miles per hour are expected, \nmainly over the Prairie Coteau. The strong winds will push Grassland \nFire Danger up to the Moderate to High category over that area, with \nthe high humidity limiting some of the fire weather concerns. \n\nThis morning into this afternoon, anticipating some drizzle from the \nlow cloud deck over parts of northeastern South Dakota and western \nMinnesota. A combination of strong humidity and upward motion in the \nlower levels along with the cloud tops generally not being cold \nenough to support cloud ice provide the necessary conditions for \ndrizzle. The southerly flow will also provide some additional \nconfidence in the drizzle potential along the western side of the \nPrairie Coteau due to upslope flow, particularly as winds shift to \nbe more southwesterly during the afternoon. There is perhaps some \nstronger signal for rain showers over parts of north central South \nDakota during the late evening to early overnight time frame, but \nonly a few hundredths of accumulation at the most is expected. \n\nAs the low pressure center departs, a cold front sweeps across the \nforecast area, dropping high temperatures into the 30s to low 40s \nover the weekend. With an upper-level low settling overhead, 850mb \ntemperatures will sit in the -10C to -5C range, right around the \n25th percentile range for this time of year. As a result, highs in \nthe 30s can be expected through at least the start of the work week. \nBeyond that point, there is a fairly consistent signal for an upper-\nlevel ridge to move overhead mid-week, which would give the region a \nslight warmup. However, probabilities currently support a fairly \nshallow ridge, raising significant questions about the extent of any \npotential warmup. \n\nThe only other chances for precipitation in the forecast come Monday \nnight into Tuesday. A shortwave trough will be the main driver \nbehind any precipitation, but overall there does not appear to be \nmuch in the way of strong forcing. An early look at soundings show \nthat the most likely precipitation type will be snow, based on lift \nthrough a saturated DGZ and a sub-freezing profile below. Given the \nlack of strong forcing being resolved, expectations are low for any \naccumulation. Mid- to long-range ensembles give a less than 20 \npercent chance for any measurable snowfall accumulation. Still, some \nareas may see the first flurries of the winter Monday night into \nTuesday.",
